DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The ccrraasshhiinnffoo utility analyzes a core dump saved by savecore(8).  It gen‐
     erates a text file containing the analysis in the same directory as the
     core dump.  For a given core dump file named _v_m_c_o_r_e_._X_X the generated text
     file will be named _c_o_r_e_._t_x_t_._X_X.

     By default, ccrraasshhiinnffoo analyzes the most recent core dump in the core dump
     directory.  A specific core dump may be specified via either the _c_o_r_e or
     _d_u_m_p_n_r arguments.  Once ccrraasshhiinnffoo has located a core dump, it analyzes
     the core dump to determine the exact version of the kernel that generated
     the core.  It then looks for a matching kernel file under each of the
     subdirectories in _/_b_o_o_t.  The location of the kernel file can also be ex‐
     plicitly provided via the _k_e_r_n_e_l argument.

     Once ccrraasshhiinnffoo has located a core dump and kernel, it uses several utili‐
     ties to analyze the core including dmesg(8), fstat(1), iostat(8),
     ipcs(1), kgdb(1) (_p_o_r_t_s_/_d_e_v_e_l_/_g_d_b), netstat(1), nfsstat(1), ps(1),
     pstat(8), and vmstat(8).  Note that kgdb must be installed from the de‐
     vel/gdb port or gdb package.

     The options are as follows:

     --bb      Run in batch mode.  Write most messages to the _c_o_r_e_._t_x_t_._X_X file
             instead of the terminal.  This flag is used when ccrraasshhiinnffoo is run
             during boot.

     --dd _c_r_a_s_h_d_i_r
             Specify an alternate core dump directory.  The default crash dump
             directory is _/_v_a_r_/_c_r_a_s_h.

     --nn _d_u_m_p_n_r
             Use the core dump saved in _v_m_c_o_r_e_._d_u_m_p_n_r instead of the latest
             core in the core dump directory.

     --kk _k_e_r_n_e_l
             Specify an explicit kernel file.

